<p>Bangalore runs on imported and packaged goods. Electronics and IT accessories land at the Kempegowda International Airport cargo terminals, containers clear at ICD Whitefield, and goods arrive through New Mangalore Port and Chennai. Across the city, D2C brands, gifting companies, sweet shops and cosmetics startups pack products for shops and online marketplaces.</p>
<p>If any of these goods are <strong>pre-packaged for sale</strong>, the business behind them needs an <strong>LMPC certificate</strong>. As a consulting firm based in Bangalore, Karnataka, Instacertify has been making LMPC certification easy for importers and packers across the city and the state, so they stay compliant while importing and selling packaged commodities. Here is what you need to know.</p>

<h2>LMPC in One Paragraph</h2>
<p>LMPC means <strong>Legal Metrology (Packaged Commodities)</strong>. Rule 27 of the Legal Metrology (Packaged Commodities) Rules, 2011 requires every <strong>manufacturer, packer and importer</strong> of pre-packaged commodities to register with the Legal Metrology authorities. The registration certificate is your LMPC certificate. It shows that your firm follows the rules on net quantity, MRP and package declarations. For the full rules, read our <a href="/resources/blogs/lmpc-certificate-registration-rules-eligibility-fee-rule27">guide to LMPC registration under Rule 27</a>.</p>

<h2>Importing Into Bangalore? Customs May Ask for Your LMPC</h2>
<p>When you import a packaged commodity from outside India, <strong>customs checks that your firm complies with the Legal Metrology rules for packaged goods</strong>. To confirm this, they may ask for your <strong>LMPC certificate</strong> along with the declarations on the packages.</p>
<p>Whether your consignment clears at the Bengaluru air cargo terminal, ICD Whitefield or a seaport, a missing registration can mean the goods wait until it is sorted out, and relabelling at the warehouse adds cost. The simple fix is to <strong>register before your first shipment</strong>. Our <a href="/resources/blogs/lmpc-compliance-requirements-importer-2026">LMPC checklist for importers</a> covers the rest.</p>

<h2>Packing in Bangalore? You Need Packer Registration</h2>
<p>LMPC is not only for importers. Under the Packaged Commodities Rules, <strong>anyone who packs or repacks goods for sale needs packer registration</strong>, even if they did not make the product. Typical Bangalore packers who need it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sweet shops and food brands</strong> packing sweets and snacks in boxes and pouches</li>
<li><strong>Eyewear sellers</strong> packing sunglasses in branded cases</li>
